## 1. 🎯 Overview & Target Outcome

### What Gets Built in M2

M2 is the first milestone where real external data enters the system. By the end of M2, a scheduled job connects to HackerOne, fetches active bug bounty programs, normalizes them, stores them in Postgres, and publishes a `scan.jobs` message to RabbitMQ for each new program.

### 🗺️ Data Flow for M2

```
HackerOne API
      │
      ▼
HackerOneCollector.fetch_listing()
      │
      ▼
ScopeParser.parse(structured_scopes)
      │
      ▼
ProgramRepository.upsert()  ──►  programs + program_scopes + program_policies (Postgres)
      │
      ▼
QueuePublisher.publish(scan.jobs)
      │
      ├── SUCCESS ──► queued_for_scan = false
      └── FAILURE ──► queued_for_scan = true ──► Reconciler retries every 5 min
```

### ✅ Definition of Done

- `POST /api/v1/scrape/trigger` results in rows in `programs` and `program_scopes`
- A message appears on `scan.jobs` in RabbitMQ management UI
- A simulated publish failure results in `queued_for_scan = true`; the reconciler clears it on next cycle
- A simulated 429 response triggers retry with `Retry-After` delay
- All tests pass, coverage ≥ 80%

### 🎯 Goals

| Goal | Description |
|---|---|
| Platform collector abstraction | Built once, extensible to BugCrowd / Intigriti |
| HackerOne collector | Fully implemented with auth, pagination, rate limit handling |
| Program normalization | Consistent `Program` model regardless of platform |
| Scope parsing | Structured `ProgramScope` objects from raw entries |
| Upsert logic | Safe re-scrape that preserves `queued_for_scan` flag |
| Queue publisher | Publishes to `scan.jobs`; sets flag on failure |
| Reconciler | Retries failed publishes every 5 minutes |
| Test coverage | ≥ 80% across all scraper modules |

